group: D2h
formula: C2H4
construction: two equivalent halves
headline: 
note: Model from extended Hückel (qualitative) · model energies in eV · calculation in Hartree · labels in D<sub>2h</sub>\n2 high empty levels drawn at the top (true energy beside them); 4 levels match the calculation only loosely (overlap < 0.80)
about: The extended Hückel diagram explains the calculation's orbitals: nothing more, nothing less.\n\n**The model.** Every column is extended Hückel (Alvarez parameters). The middle column is the molecule's own extended Hückel run, the side columns are the two fragments' (CH2 and CH2). The basis is the same in all three, so the lines are an exact projection of each model MO on the fragment orbitals: a line joins a model MO to a fragment level that makes up at least 10 % of it.\n\n**Bonding and antibonding** are read from the sign of each model MO's overlap population between the two fragments, then paired per symmetry label and degeneracy, so each antibonding level has its bonding partner; a level without a partner is non-bonding. Colours mark the pairs.\n\n**The calculation** is the column on the right, on its own energy axis in Hartree and to scale. For each model level it shows the calculated orbital (or set of near-degenerate orbitals) with the largest overlap with it, at its calculated energy, in the colour of the model level it matches. A level marked ≈ matches only loosely (overlap below 0.80; the value is in the hover text). Labels are those of the matched calculated orbital. Clicking a level shows that orbital in 3-D.\n\nThe order of some empty levels above the LUMO differs between the model and your calculation. Virtual orbitals above the LUMO depend strongly on the basis set.\n\n**Total electrons: 12** (CH2 6, CH2 6).\n\n**Caveats.** 2 high empty levels drawn at the top (true energy beside them); 4 levels match the calculation only loosely (overlap < 0.80). Extended Hückel puts empty antibonding orbitals tens of eV up, so an empty level more than 30 eV above the HOMO is drawn at the top of the axis with its model energy written beside it, or not at all when it sits almost entirely on one fragment. A loose match means no calculated orbital resembles the model level closely; read its calculated energy with care.\n\n**Orbital labels.** Symmetry labels computed from the orbitals in D2h; the code reported none.  b1 and b2 (b2 and b3 in D2h) depend on the choice of axes; ECCE follows Mulliken's convention, as most textbooks do.\n\n**Picked atoms.** One atom is drawn against the atoms bonded to it; two bonded atoms cut that bond and the two sides are drawn against each other (in and out of phase when they are equal). A metal gives the fragment-orbital diagram, each ligand being a connected piece left when the metal is removed.
verdict: differs The model puts 1b3g* below 3ag; your calculation has them the other way round (1 of 21 pairs of the 7 levels judged).
